ge; a self-loop is one.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"An edge to a finished vertex is a forward edge when that vertex was discovered later than the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex being explored, and a cross edge otherwise.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In a directed graph every out-edge of an explored vertex is reported. In an undirected graph"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each edge is reported once, from the end that reaches it first: the tree edge is not"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"reported again from the vertex it discovered, but a parallel edge to the parent is a back edge,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and a self-loop is one back edge. Without pruning or a depth limit an undirected search has"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"only tree and back edges; an edge to a finished vertex whose edges were not explored is a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"forward or cross edge."}]}]}],"references":{"doc://Traversal/documentation/Traversal/EdgeDepthFirstSearchEvent/Equatable-Implementations":{"role":"collectionGroup","title":"Equatable Implementations","abstract":[],"url":"\/documentation\/traversal\/edgedepthfirstsearchevent\/equatable-implementations","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Traversal\/documentation\/Traversal\/EdgeDepthFirstSearchEvent\/Equatable-Implementations","kind":"article"},"doc://Traversal/documentation/Traversal/EdgeDepthFirstSearchEvent/discover(_:)":{"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Traversal\/documentation\/Traversal\/EdgeDepthFirstSearchEvent\/discover(_:)","type":"topic","abstract":[],"title":"EdgeDepthFirstSearchEvent.discover(_:)","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"discover"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Vertex"},{"kind":"t